item.description:
 &lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I spent several months thinking about this and (sorry, all you AI haterz) a little over fifteen minutes with Claude drafting it: a model bill to hobble corruption on the Supreme Court by (1) requiring complete financial disclosure from all SC justices for their full tenure to date plus the five preceding years, and (2) sending any findings that a justice knowingly and willfully refused to comply, or filed materially false or incomplete information, to Congress for possible impeachment. We can do this!&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And — I &lt;em&gt;really&lt;/em&gt; shouldn’t need to add — this is as open-source as it gets, so do whatever you like with it. IOW &lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;share and enjoy!&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Congress may require justices to disclose their finances and may state its view that knowing falsification is impeachable, but it cannot make impeachment automatic. These sections are drafted around that line.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;section-1-short-title-and-findings&quot;&gt;Section 1. Short title and findings&lt;a role=&quot;anchor&quot; aria-hidden tabindex=&quot;-1&quot; data-no-popover href=&quot;#section-1-short-title-and-findings&quot; class=&quot;internal&quot;&gt;&lt;svg width=&quot;18&quot; height=&quot;18&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 24 24&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot; stroke=&quot;currentColor&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot; stroke-linecap=&quot;round&quot; stroke-linejoin=&quot;round&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(a) Short title.&lt;/strong&gt; This Act may be cited as the “Supreme Court Transparency, Oversight, and Probity (STOP) Corruption Act.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(b) Findings.&lt;/strong&gt; Congress finds the following:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Article III, section 1 provides that Justices of the Supreme Court hold their offices during good behavior.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Public confidence in the Supreme Court depends on accurate and complete public knowledge of the financial interests, gifts, and reimbursements of its Justices.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Title I of the Ethics in Government Act of 1978 has long required financial disclosure by Justices and other Article III judges, and the Justices have complied with it, while the Court has not decided whether Congress may impose those requirements on it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Congress has enacted laws governing the Court’s size, budget, jurisdiction, quorum, oath, and recusal, and may exercise its power under Article I, section 8, clause 18 to enact reasonable disclosure requirements that do not impair the Court’s exercise of its judicial power.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A reporting requirement does not decide any case, direct any judgment, or restrict the Court’s constitutional functions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Existing procedures for reviewing the disclosures of Justices have not consistently produced timely and complete public reporting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(c) Sense of Congress.&lt;/strong&gt; It is the sense of Congress that the knowing and willful submission of a materially false financial disclosure report, or the knowing and willful refusal to file a required report after notice and an opportunity to cure, by a Justice of the Supreme Court may constitute a high crime or misdemeanor within the meaning of Article II, section 4 of the Constitution. Nothing in this subsection determines whether any particular conduct warrants impeachment, which is committed by the Constitution to the House of Representatives and the Senate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;section-2-definitions-and-scope-of-disclosure&quot;&gt;Section 2. Definitions and scope of disclosure&lt;a role=&quot;anchor&quot; aria-hidden tabindex=&quot;-1&quot; data-no-popover href=&quot;#section-2-definitions-and-scope-of-disclosure&quot; class=&quot;internal&quot;&gt;&lt;svg width=&quot;18&quot; height=&quot;18&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 24 24&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot; stroke=&quot;currentColor&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot; stroke-linecap=&quot;round&quot; stroke-linejoin=&quot;round&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(a) Definitions.&lt;/strong&gt; In this Act:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Covered Justice&lt;/strong&gt; means the Chief Justice and each Associate Justice of the Supreme Court serving on the date of enactment, and each person appointed afterward.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Covered period&lt;/strong&gt; means, for each Covered Justice, the period beginning five years before the date on which the Justice took the judicial oath as a Justice of the Supreme Court and ending on the date of the report.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reportable item&lt;/strong&gt; means any item required to be reported by a judicial officer under section 13104 of title 5, United States Code, including income, gifts, reimbursements, travel and hospitality, assets, transactions, liabilities, positions, and agreements, together with the items listed in subsection (c).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Panel&lt;/strong&gt; means the review panel established under section 4(b).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(b) Initial comprehensive report.&lt;/strong&gt; Not later than 180 days after the date of enactment, each Covered Justice shall file with the Administrative Office of the United States Courts a report covering all reportable items for the period beginning on the date of the Justice’s oath. Not later than 12 months after the date of enactment, each Covered Justice shall file a supplemental report covering the five years before that oath. The Administrative Office may grant one extension of up to 90 days for good cause. A Justice appointed after enactment shall file both reports within 12 months after taking the judicial oath.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(c) Additional items.&lt;/strong&gt; The report shall also include, for the covered period:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the value of any in-kind benefit, including use of aircraft, vessels, or lodging, from any person other than a family member, regardless of whether it qualifies as a gift under other law;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;any interest in a private entity in which the Justice, or the Justice’s spouse or dependent child, held an ownership or income interest of more than [$1,000]; and&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;any sale or transfer of real property to a person or entity that was a party or amicus curiae in a case decided by the Court within three years of the transaction, as shown on the Court’s public docket.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(d) Spouses and dependents.&lt;/strong&gt; Reporting for a spouse or dependent child shall follow section 13104 of title 5, United States Code, and need not include information that the Justice certifies in good faith is not known and cannot reasonably be obtained.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(e) Security redactions.&lt;/strong&gt; The Administrative Office shall redact information from public posting to the extent the Judicial Conference determines that disclosure would endanger the safety of a Justice or a family member, consistent with the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022. The Administrative Office shall preserve and make available to the Panel the unredacted report.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(f) Annual reports and public posting.&lt;/strong&gt; After the initial report, each Covered Justice shall file annual reports under existing law. The Administrative Office shall post each report, as redacted under subsection (e), on a searchable public website within 90 days after filing.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(g) Pre-appointment years.&lt;/strong&gt; The portion of any report covering years before appointment is a report of historical information. No penalty attaches to the content of any pre-appointment transaction itself, only to a refusal to report it or a false report about it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;section-3-certification-and-referral-to-congress&quot;&gt;Section 3. Certification and referral to Congress&lt;a role=&quot;anchor&quot; aria-hidden tabindex=&quot;-1&quot; data-no-popover href=&quot;#section-3-certification-and-referral-to-congress&quot; class=&quot;internal&quot;&gt;&lt;svg width=&quot;18&quot; height=&quot;18&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 24 24&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot; stroke=&quot;currentColor&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot; stroke-linecap=&quot;round&quot; stroke-linejoin=&quot;round&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(a) Grounds for certification.&lt;/strong&gt; The Panel shall issue a Certified Finding of Willful Noncompliance regarding a Covered Justice if, after the procedure in section 4, it determines by clear and convincing evidence that the Justice:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;failed to file a report required by this Act within 30 days after written notice of a missed deadline; or&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;knowingly and willfully made a materially false statement or omitted a material item in a report required by this Act, and did not correct the report under section 4(d).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(b) Transmission.&lt;/strong&gt; Within 30 days after a Certified Finding becomes final under section 4(g), the Panel shall transmit it, with the complete record, to the Speaker of the House of Representatives, the Chair and Ranking Member of the Committee on the Judiciary of the House, and the Majority and Minority Leaders and the Chair and Ranking Member of the Committee on the Judiciary of the Senate. The Panel shall make a redacted version public within 30 days after transmission.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(c) Effect of certification.&lt;/strong&gt; A Certified Finding:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;is a report to Congress and is not a determination that any conduct is a high crime or misdemeanor;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;does not remove, suspend, or discipline a Justice, does not affect the Justice’s authority to participate in any case, and does not alter the validity of any decision of the Court; and&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;does not require either House to take any action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(d) Separate remedies preserved.&lt;/strong&gt; Nothing in this section limits civil or criminal enforcement of the disclosure laws under other provisions of law, including section 13106 of title 5, United States Code.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(e) Failure of the Panel to act.&lt;/strong&gt; [Option A: If the Panel has not issued a determination within 270 days after receiving a complaint that meets the requirements of section 4(a), the Panel shall transmit the complaint and its record to the committees listed in subsection (b) with a statement of the reason for the delay.] [Option B: omit.]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;section-4-notice-response-cure-and-record&quot;&gt;Section 4. Notice, response, cure, and record&lt;a role=&quot;anchor&quot; aria-hidden tabindex=&quot;-1&quot; data-no-popover href=&quot;#section-4-notice-response-cure-and-record&quot; class=&quot;internal&quot;&gt;&lt;svg width=&quot;18&quot; height=&quot;18&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 24 24&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot; stroke=&quot;currentColor&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot; stroke-linecap=&quot;round&quot; stroke-linejoin=&quot;round&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(a) Complaints.&lt;/strong&gt; The Administrative Office, the Judicial Conference’s Committee on Financial Disclosure, or any person may submit a written complaint to the Panel identifying the report and the alleged omission or falsity with reasonable specificity. The Panel may dismiss a complaint that does not meet this standard.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(b) Review panel.&lt;/strong&gt; The Panel shall consist of three members selected by lot from a roster of senior circuit judges and retired Justices of the Supreme Court who consent to serve, none of whom has a financial, familial, or employment relationship with the Justice under review. No sitting Justice may serve on the Panel or participate in selecting its members. [Alternative: the Panel’s functions are performed by the Judicial Conference’s Committee on Financial Disclosure, with the referral in section 3(e) if it fails to act.]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(c) Notice and response.&lt;/strong&gt; Before any finding, the Panel shall give the Justice written notice stating each alleged violation and the evidence relied on. The Justice shall have 45 days to respond in writing, to submit evidence, and to request a hearing. The Panel shall hold a hearing if requested, with the right to counsel and to present and examine witnesses.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(d) Cure of honest error.&lt;/strong&gt; A Justice who files a corrected report within the response period in subsection (c), or who files an amendment before receiving notice of a complaint, shall not be subject to a finding under section 3(a)(2) on the basis of the omitted or incorrect item. Filing a corrected report is not an admission of willfulness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(e) Standard and record.&lt;/strong&gt; The Panel shall make findings of fact and conclusions of law in a written decision stating each basis for any finding. The decision shall be supported by clear and convincing evidence. A mistake, inadvertence, or good-faith reliance on professional advice is not willful.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(f) Confidentiality.&lt;/strong&gt; Proceedings shall be confidential until a Certified Finding is issued or the complaint is dismissed, except as needed to satisfy section 3(e).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(g) Review.&lt;/strong&gt; Before a Certified Finding becomes final, the Justice may request review of the decision on the record by a second panel constituted under subsection (b), whose members did not serve on the first. Review is limited to whether the Panel followed this section and whether the finding is supported by clear and convincing evidence. The second panel shall decide within 60 days. A Certified Finding is not otherwise subject to judicial review.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;section-5-expedited-consideration-construction-effective-date-and-severability&quot;&gt;Section 5. Expedited consideration, construction, effective date, and severability&lt;a role=&quot;anchor&quot; aria-hidden tabindex=&quot;-1&quot; data-no-popover href=&quot;#section-5-expedited-consideration-construction-effective-date-and-severability&quot; class=&quot;internal&quot;&gt;&lt;svg width=&quot;18&quot; height=&quot;18&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 24 24&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot; stroke=&quot;currentColor&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot; stroke-linecap=&quot;round&quot; stroke-linejoin=&quot;round&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(a) Rulemaking of each House.&lt;/strong&gt; This subsection is enacted as an exercise of the rulemaking power of the House of Representatives and the Senate, with full recognition of the constitutional right of either House to change its rules at any time. Each Certified Finding transmitted under section 3(b) shall be referred to the Committee on the Judiciary of the House, which shall, within 60 days, report to the House whether it will initiate an inquiry and, if not, the reasons. Nothing in this subsection limits the power of either House to determine its own proceedings.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(b) Rule of construction.&lt;/strong&gt; Nothing in this Act:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;limits the power of the House of Representatives to impeach, or of the Senate to try, any officer of the United States;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;establishes, defines, or limits the grounds for impeachment or removal; or&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;conditions a Justice’s tenure on compliance with this Act, other than through the constitutional impeachment process.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(c) Effective date and application.&lt;/strong&gt; This Act takes effect on the date of enactment. Penalties and findings under section 3 apply only to a failure to file, or a false statement or omission, occurring after the date of enactment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;(d) Severability.&lt;/strong&gt; If any provision of this Act, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of the Act, and its application to other persons or circumstances, shall not be affected. In particular, invalidity of section 2(a)(2) as applied to the five years before appointment shall not affect the requirements for the period after appointment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;[Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid0BcWHbpb4zxB2fd5erXV7rGL2eCvM6vnnsYu326hCSgzF8us3zMQvVCfTf73P9SJgl&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;FB&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Painting of Mar Saba to make you look.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../blg/new/new_images/2026-10-27-the-israeli-election.jpg&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My hot take on the Israeli election: Netanyahu being ejected from office is the necessary precondition for any positive change. It isn’t sufficient, not even close; and the other leading parties are disasters in their own right. But nothing good can happen while he remains in office, so ejecting him is the first step — and, behind and beneath the coalition-building process (which could drag on for months) quite a few movers and shakers will be looking for ways out.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;American commentators can be ignored almost en bloc because the US’s two-party system is, in many ways, antithetical to parliamentary systems, which are much more subtle and fluid. Moreover, a &lt;em&gt;huge&lt;/em&gt; segment of the people weighing in will be far more concerned with performing their own “antizionism” than making well-informed, well-considered, relevant observations. So, for example, when the entire Levant doesn’t morph overnight into some idyllic landscape, they’ll complain bitterly that no change is possible. In a sense, that’s true — the change &lt;em&gt;they&lt;/em&gt; want isn’t possible — but their demands, however virtuous, have no basis in reality. Or at least no more than the US deciding to repatriate a major urban center to the Native Americans who were driven out of it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Unfortunately, there are very, very few people who have a sophisticated grasp on Israeli politics but no inherited skin in that game — one of the downsides of a religious ethnostate, I guess. So most of the deeply informed commentaries we &lt;em&gt;do&lt;/em&gt; see in the ~West will be biased one way or another. That, then, is the challenge: maintaining a hope that’s grounded in fact without getting played, whether deliberately or not.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All of this will be additionally complicated by developments elsewhere: Trump &amp;#x26; Co’s grip on power will slip and rats of every kind will be fleeing from their sinking ship as officials do now — by disguising themselves as talking heads. The Democratic Party will be consumed by internal fights, fueled in no small part by pro-Israel ultras — who will continue to threaten to defect to the GOP, and in doing so align themselves with an objectively fascist party. Much of Europe will be moving in a nearly opposite direction, embracing increasingly rightist and even fascist parties and locked in struggles to position their countries and multilaterals WRT Ukraine and Russia. And Iran? Who even knows how they’ll navigate this, except using their growing leverage to f— the US, Israel, and some of their key allies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There are other immense unknowns, too: economies spinning out of control, the mounting impact of Trumpian policies, Iranian power, and Ukrainian agriculture on key commodities (mainly oil and fertilizer), China’s rise as the 21C’s default superpower. None of these things point toward any clear resolution of Israel’s status and role.&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;[Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid02sRRbPQJ5A3G68b4AZD6orEPx8tecADofVsJodBstFFGqzi9Et6SReRFDvFUjhmdkl&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;FB&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/reel/1739212947166611&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;All very well and good if you assume that the sole function of universities is to teach&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt; — but it isn’t. There are a few other minor matters like:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;research, which involves everything from coordination (long-term strategy, staffing plans, facilities planning from increasingly technical buildings to equipment acquisitions and maintenance), funding (everything from grant-writing to ongoing compliance to final reporting);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;360° compliance (legal, HR, finance, facilities maintenance, ongoing oversight, remediation);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;facilities (community liaising, planning, construction, maintenance, coordination, security, coordination with local law enforcement, materials and inventory);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;student services (dorm operations, dining halls and food hygiene, health services including emergency planning and coordination), activities, institutional schedule planning, communications);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;financial aid (high-stakes, unstable under the best of circumstances, compliance-intensive, emergency issues, etc);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;faculty-oriented services (strategy, planning, coordination, hiring, assessment, compliance, remediation);&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;curricular operations (long-term strategy, implementation, assignment, assessment, review, accreditation).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;IT is a whole other kettle of fish that pervades every one of these things, from long-term planning, to reliable operations, to acquisitions and maintenance, to security, to compliance — and that doesn’t even touch &lt;em&gt;content&lt;/em&gt;. Oh, and then there’s marketing and promotions, which schools live or die by. This list could go on and on and on: trustees or regents, alumnae/i relations basic rituals like convocation and commencement. I think sports needs to go, but that’s a minority position, so there’s all that too: strategy, facilities planning and maintenance, coordination, scheduling, transportation, materials, as well as liability issues, promotions, etc, etc. Some of this could be slimmed down, but the fact is that in a modern university &lt;em&gt;teaching&lt;/em&gt; is a pretty trivial line item. If you want to go back to simpler times, that could happen, but you can kiss off every aspect of social equity. So, in the absence of a coherent, concrete vision of a stripped-down, back-to-basics, teaching-driven university, these “admin bloat” critiques are mostly bullshit. They don’t have to be, but they are.&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;[Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid0382nYZuHQNvEW5oYLokCSbKx2ZxtDcTL2hfruk4TCRVyCMVpF8ttLD9EJjkRdcrnfl&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;FB&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Today’s Imma-up-this goes out to AH and AK, in response to &lt;a href=&quot;https://lists.aoir.org/archives/list/air-l@lists.aoir.org/thread/KAO7FUOOJGSZN4XAM7BSDDDWQKAAQREU/&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;the AI bot on the AoIR list&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I’m quickly coming to the conclusion that critiques of AI originating from the ~humanities are intellectually bankrupt, for the most part — basically reactionary special pleading “on stilts,” as the saying goes. That’s not to say they’re substantively wrong or morally misdirected; it is to say that a stopped clock (or maybe &lt;em&gt;slopped&lt;/em&gt; clock) can be right twice a day. I just don’t believe that they’re deeply rooted in principle rather than in, say, fear, frustration, or ressentiment. More specifically, while I sympathize with people’s frustration at reply-all meltdowns (though was it actually one of those? seems unlikely), it kinda boggles the mind that INTERNET RESEARCHERS would react to such a novel phenomenon — and, at least on its face, surprisingly candid — with histrionic pearl-clutching, tantrums, “fandom flounces,” and misc internet drama nonsense. And I don’t say that from some airy-fairy abstraction, I moderated nettime for 25 F-NG YEARS, which is the equivalent of having survived the Somme, Verdun, Passchendaele, Gallipoli, &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; Ypres. 😹 And nor are these issues in past. When the wave of AI bots requesting accounts on our mastodon instance became a tsunami and the other mods started to lose it, I suggested simply de-automating the process and actually, you know, talking with people — which turned out to be win-win-win. AoIR is a different kettle of fish, because nettime was, from the beginning, reflexively and critically concerned with the power relations of open/closed/moderated/etc. But imagine for a moment if the list had been able to say “hold off on your reactions, and let’s try to have a civil chat with this thing to see what good might come of it,” that would have been &lt;em&gt;so&lt;/em&gt; much more interesting. And more in keeping with what we’ll all need to learn to do, like it or not.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;[Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid0382nYZuHQNvEW5oYLokCSbKx2ZxtDcTL2hfruk4TCRVyCMVpF8ttLD9EJjkRdcrnfl&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;FB&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../blg/new/new_images/2026-10-12-jacobin-and-the-missing-decades-of-the-paperback.jpg&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I don’t really want to pick on &lt;em&gt;Jacobin&lt;/em&gt; too much, but how in T living F do you &lt;a href=&quot;https://jacobin.com/2026/10/books-publishing-reading-mass-market-paperback&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;write a potted cultural history of the rise and fall of the paperback from the late 1930s to now without &lt;em&gt;once&lt;/em&gt; mentioning the 1950s, 1960s, 1980s, 1990s, 2000s, or 2010s?&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt; I left out the 1970s because 1979 gets mentioned once! — as the high point of paperback sales (see below for why things collapsed after that). Just “for many decades,” “decades-long…binge,” and “intervening decades,” IOW I-can’t-be-arsed handwaving. TV and radio get one mention each, but only as metonyms for some magical e-media-free landscape. Newspapers? Nope. Magazines? Nope. The latter are especially important in this context because their ebbs and flows say a &lt;em&gt;lot&lt;/em&gt; about America’s reading habits — for ex, the explosion of mag titles from the early 1960s on, the rise of “new journalism,” and the death of several old standards in 1971, plus or minus. Cable TV? Not a mention. Movies get a few, again mostly as foils. THE INTERNET? Not explicitly, just as the unnamed presence behind “school-issued personal video devices.” Textbooks? Nope. Distribution? A few cursory comments. Libraries? Once — and not in the context of hardcovers, so no mention of institutional sales (essential for understanding hard- vs softcover market dynamics). If that all weren’t enough, the &lt;em&gt;key&lt;/em&gt; material development he left out was the rise of the “quality” — larger format —paperback in the 1970s. How large? The same size as hardcovers! Why? Because the only difference was the binding! Why? Because selling cheap paperbacks wasn’t as financially sustainable as selling the same interiors &lt;em&gt;in different wrappers&lt;/em&gt;. (Ah, right: other things that get no mention: “finance,” “economics,” “profit,” “loss”…but I digress. 😹) Another consideration that merits no mention: printing.  Ask me about the rising costs of it — in a former life, I was a print broker for some small presses in the US and Latin America.) And don’t &lt;em&gt;even&lt;/em&gt; get me going on the Supreme Court ruling that demolished the core biz model that sustained the cheap paperback biz, which was (1) print zillions of copies and warehouse them, (2) write the whole cost off, (3) sell the tax-free inventory for decades to come. Until &lt;em&gt;Thor Power Tool Co. v. Commissioner&lt;/em&gt;, the 1979 ruling that transformed how businesses could account for inventory. That was when publishers cost-shifted inventory to distributors, who didn’t GAF about content: if a title didn’t sell, they pulped it. After that, backlists — which previously had been inventory — just became IP. And &lt;em&gt;that&lt;/em&gt;, my friends, is why paperbacks died from 1980 on. I’d love to applaud this piece and &lt;em&gt;Jacobin&lt;/em&gt; for running it, but I just can’t.&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;[Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid02dB9961yF7asuGKpJ8VFs4cEydmAdEH6ShnvtbidxmA95uwnA5usHbu1FtJjfqkc1l&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;FB&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../blg/new/new_images/2026-10-10-ai-in-the-lineage-of-the-photocopier.jpg&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you fetishize AI as somehow categorically different from other “technologies,” then (I mean, hypocrisies aside) this is Very Wrong. But if, instead, you see AI as consistent with a narrower technical lineage — which includes photocopiers, faxes, word processors (hardware and software), desktop publishing, OCR, print on demand are some of the most obvious ones — then (a) it makes sense, (b) is inevitable, and (c) will therefore be come to be seen as necessary and right. There are &lt;em&gt;some&lt;/em&gt; layers of copy/sub-editing and even more so of proofreading that will be done better by AIs than by people, period (or “full stop,” as some would have it) — mainly the repetitive drudgery aspect. For now, that assumes the AI is used by someone with practical experience; but as AI providers learn from interaction, it’ll become trivial to bake those higher-order functions into the software itself. Either way, generic debates about this won’t make much sense if they ignore the overarching fact that there are different kinds of publishing, which will be affected differently. One kind privileges the pipeline so it imposes ever-growing strictures on “writing” (technical, academic, reference, etc) — this will get eaten up by AI. The other kind is more speculative and open (everything from chapbooks to ‘independent’ ventures of every kind; that stuff is so arbitrary that AI won’t be able to automate it anytime soon, but it &lt;em&gt;will&lt;/em&gt; be able to ~simulate it. But one BIG problem that AI poses for publishing, as it does for so many other fields of activity, is that it’ll slam the door on entry-level work: in its desperate effort to make ends meet &lt;em&gt;now&lt;/em&gt; (even if those ends are the demands of private equity), the industry will starve itself of new talent. And, of course, none of these defensive/reactive discussions ask one crucial question: what will the benefits be?&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;Trump trying to rename things is boring, and people griping about it is even more boring. Here’s why:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Nothing in this world has one and only one name, least of all mappable things, which are — by definition — social and political objects. Names for these things vary by language, nationality, region, and every other major distinction you can think of. The United States of America is &lt;em&gt;also&lt;/em&gt; Estados Unidos de América, États-Unis d’Amérique, 美国, الولايات المتحدة الأمريكية, and Соединённые Штаты Америки. Some of these names are literal translations, others not. For example, the Viet name for the USA is Hoa Kỳ, which means “flowery flag” (as in the stars).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Google and Apple know where users are and they change map labels based on that. People in China, France, Brazil, or Pakistan see maps in the appropriate language(s). On a finer-grained level, people in different anglophone countries are shown maps with the appropriate variations in orthography variations (“color” vs “colour,” “center” vs “centre”) and idiom (“freeway” vs “motorway,” “parking lot” vs “car park,” etc). That’s normal.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In practice, that means these map services are perfectly capable of calling it “Lake America” for US users and “Lake Ontario” for Canadian users. And, in fact, through extensive experimentation — I searched Google Maps for the two names from a US IP address and then from a Canadian IP address — I’ve determined that that’s exactly what Google does. Canadians see “Lake Ontario” and Americans see “Lake America.” I can’t be arsed to reproduce experiment this using Apple Maps &lt;em&gt;because it’s dumb and boring&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../ci-img/Screenshot-2026-09-03-at-14.37.04.png&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;wmed relative|center&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../ci-img/Screenshot-2026-09-03-at-14.36.11.png&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;wmed relative|center&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So, basically, Americans are upset that American companies “obeyed-in-advance” (remember that quaint phrase?) the American president’s diktat. His announcement carried no legal force, but — as we’ve seen — his administration aggressively pursues entities and people it perceives as obstructing it. Google and Apple could have taken a heroic stand and become embroiled in a transient non-issue, but instead they did exactly what their shareholders would want them to do: protect their investments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you’re upset by this, your issue isn’t really with Google or Apple, it’s with Trump, or with the GOP, or with capitalism. And of all the myriad things that Trump &amp;#x26; Co are doing, this kind of imaginary renaming of geological features doesn’t matter. Offensive? Absolutely. Delusional? Without a doubt. But it won’t kill people, it won’t destroy essential institutions, and it isn’t irreversible. On the contrary: when Trump crosses that rainbow bridge, which will be soon enough, these companies will quietly revert these changes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What Trump did is dumb and boring, and getting worked up over it instead of something that matters is even dumber or boringer. So I hope you’ll consider finding something more significant and permanent to get worked up over.&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;[Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid02uiDwScQvDcJy6PqbgC6Q9UoagE14FkWgQY8cUtUW2Cue7QeqKVL3Qzr8TjMFCCo6l&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;FB&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../blg/new/new_images/2026-09-03-ending-the-world-as-a-check-and-balance.jpg&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This morning’s Imma-up-this goes out to &lt;a href=&quot;https://law.stanford.edu/mark-a-lemley/&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;Mark Lemley&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;People can secular-snark all they like, but president-adjacent figures have conventionally treated the risk of &lt;em&gt;bringing about the end of the world&lt;/em&gt; as the ultimate check-and-balance — something most of us regard as a good thing. In pop terms, this is &lt;em&gt;literal&lt;/em&gt; (or, better, &lt;em&gt;cartoonish&lt;/em&gt;) supervillain stuff: kill everyone for private gain! But understood a little more formally it’s terrifying, because it inverts traditional feedback loops like &lt;em&gt;deterrence&lt;/em&gt;. When an advisor comes to the president and says “Sir, doing X would risk nuclear war,” the correct response is &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; for the president to rub his hands together gleefully and mutter “&lt;em&gt;excellent…&lt;/em&gt;”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;JDV has made a lot of hay about converting to Catholicism — a denomination that hasn’t been associated with apocalypticism in a &lt;em&gt;very&lt;/em&gt; long time. This kind of millenarian noise he’s generating is much more associated with Protestantism and, in particular, evangelical sects. So this fusion he’s spouting is dangerous in several ways. Right when the evangelical movement is finally losing some steam, this kind of effort to reinforce it with reactionary Catholicism is not what we need &lt;em&gt;at all&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It also reveals another way in which Trumpism has become institutionalized. In the past, it was absolutely mandatory for elected officials at that level to show respect for clergies and, in particular, the Vatican. No more: now that they’ve cast the pope and liberal clergy members as “woke,” anything goes — with the feedback loop going in the wrong direction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I’m not religious, but I studied some theology, and that’s pretty much the working definition of evil I arrived at: making the machinery of good run in reverse. Officials who are actively, deliberately, and explicitly evil are a growing problem, and I don’t think our messy mix of elections and judicial review is up to the task of dealing effectively with them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;Bets to treat this meditation on &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luther_Blissett_(pseudonym)&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;Luther Blissett&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt; as self-explanatory&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;video src=&quot;../../files/luther-blissett-fantasy-footballer-2004.mp4&quot; controls&gt;&lt;/video&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I was promoted to post this today by this odd apparition (🎩 Tim Mitchell): an image, attributed to the not-really-existent “Andy Warhol &amp;#x26; Co. Ltd.” (Warhol died in 1987) rendition of a composite image generated in 1994 by Andrea Alberti and Edi Bianco by morphing two pre-WW2 portraits, to create a synthetic avatar for the synthetic “open pseudonym” &lt;a href=&quot;https://web.archive.org/web/20080421172856/http://www.t-h-e-n-e-t.com/html/_film/pers/_pers_blisset.htm&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;Luther Blissett&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;em&gt;See also:&lt;/em&gt; Marco Deseriis’s &lt;a href=&quot;https://books.google.com/books?id=1ih0DwAAQBAJ&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;Improper Names: Collective Pseudonyms from the Luddites to Anonymous&lt;/em&gt;&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (Minneapolis: University of Minnesota Press, 2015).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../ci-img/2026-08-19_luther-blissett-by-not-warhol.jpg&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;wmed relative|center&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The “original”:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../ci-img/2026-08-19_luther_blissett.jpg&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;wmed relative|center&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../ci-img/preweb-flyers/preweb-nyc-flyers-1-sm.png&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;wmed relative|center&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
