 &lt;p&gt;(Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid021ssjyRfJWU69SHSNDFivXVFe5rqXvBgzy6wdwK3rEUyq6mg7YGLvUXoMdP4EKSBrl&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;FB&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Andrea Pitzer’s video piece &lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/W3aMgiR9mMI&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;What counts as a concentration camp?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt; isn’t too long (24 mins) and is really interesting — in part for how it’s edited, which feels like the video equivalent of a rough cut of an academic essay. Like most tendentious exercises in applied neoplatonism, the debate about whether Trump &amp;#x26; Co are building “concentration camps” is tiresome, artificial, and unnecessary; and arguments that they aren’t are starting to smell increasingly suspect. Pitzer’s history of CCs, &lt;em&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://isbn.nu/978-0316303590&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;One Long Night&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, is very good, and it’s hardly the first of its kind. Sid Mintz’s history of plantations, &lt;em&gt;Sweetness and Power&lt;/em&gt;, traces a similar trajectory of how that form was developed through experimentation over centuries. There is &lt;em&gt;no&lt;/em&gt; fixed, clearly bounded model of a CC. On the contrary, as Pitzer argues, CCs are best understood as a &lt;em&gt;process&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There was a time (and, arguably, reason) to assert that certain words and phrases — notably &lt;em&gt;concentration camp&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;genocide&lt;/em&gt; — had particular origins and associations that discourages using them widely. Under Netanyahu and Co, Israel has pissed away the last vestiges of that exceptionalism; and those of Israel’s supporters who’ve failed to responsibly criticize its excesses have relinquished any right to claim some sort of ownership of those terms. Those claims, well past their sell-by date, appear under a new sign, as efforts to control discussion and debate — and, as such, are part and parcel with efforts to intimidate, silence, and persecute effective critics of Israel’s policies and practices.&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;[Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;../../[FB](https:/www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid02cZYCHwviTsBuYx6QDtSdWoLYZbmXJM9yPyNtg4izT65XLW8qTq7PnfFjPZrSrXccl)&quot; class=&quot;internal alias&quot; data-slug=&quot;[FB](https:/www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid02cZYCHwviTsBuYx6QDtSdWoLYZbmXJM9yPyNtg4izT65XLW8qTq7PnfFjPZrSrXccl)&quot;&gt;FB&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Imma up this from my comment because it’s pretty esoteric knowledge:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I was on cypherpunks from ~1993 when it had a ~250 people and joined the spinoff cryptography mailing list early on, maybe in 2002 — very soon after 9/11, I think, because Perry Metzger worked on Wall St. So by the time Satoshi published his paper in 2008, I was thoroughly steeped in all that — enough to (I’m &lt;em&gt;still&lt;/em&gt; pissed) decide not to install BTC because it ran on java, which I’d grown to hate (ironically, in large part &lt;em&gt;because&lt;/em&gt; it had been so deeply hated by the CP milieu for so long). So, yes, I watched all those ideas unfold over many years, to the point where the surprise of BTC was only that someone had integrated so many of those ideas into a single project.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As for a single person, well, BitTorrent was just one initially (Bram Cohen) and Wikileaks was basically one — Assange, although the domain was originally registered by John Young, who parted ways with JA early on because JA was a psycho PITA. John, who ran &lt;a href=&quot;../../Cryptome&quot; class=&quot;internal&quot; data-slug=&quot;Cryptome&quot;&gt;Cryptome&lt;/a&gt; until he died last year, was an ’68er architect of all things who lived on NYC’s Upper West Side, and we were friends.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I can say with some certainty that the stylometry arguments are all bullshit — it was reflexively understood in that milieu that peculiarities could give the game away. The overlapping interests args are a slightly higher grade of BS, but they’d need to operate on the level of oral/intellectual history to tease out significant differences that could ID someone. The strongest args are the long-term traffic analysis, when Satoshi comes and others come and go. That’s the kind of thing it’s much, much harder for an individual to systematically manipulate or fake. On that basis, and &lt;em&gt;only&lt;/em&gt; on that basis, would I accept the claim that Adam Back was Satoshi.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So, all of that said, do state actors know who he is? Interesting question! Short answer: I think the answer has morphed from probably not to probably. Basically, ML and AI trained on all the relevant corpuses (mailing list archives, conf proceedings, journalistic refs, books, corporate papers, patents, repositories, etc) will make it harder and harder for Satoshi to hide (unless he’s dead). In that respect, this NYT article might be a sort of a tipping point.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Luftgründer&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, lit. “air-founder”: (1) a Silly Valley–style aspirational “Founder” with unrealistic expectations about the sheer mass of effort it takes to maintain anything — say, like a government. (2) A more accurate name for “libertarian.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../2026-04-08-luftgründer.jpeg&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;wmed relative|center&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Context &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid0A9XRYbA89ELsgZ8QvCjXqSV96SeuaimmCG6sjZKwE8DovxZ11G8xtLK7Gsdf3cUQl&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;here&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, prompted by the &lt;em&gt;NYT&lt;/em&gt; purporting to out [Adam Back as Satoshi](https: //&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/08/business/bitcoin-satoshi-nakamoto-identity-adam-back.html)(&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;www.nytimes.com/2026/04/08/business/bitcoin-satoshi-nakamoto-identity-adam-back.html)(&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;?!?!).&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;Apparently, pretty much no one has ever typed the phrase “Klaus Gnomi” before, so here it is and I’ll reserve it — maybe for a pet name, maybe a Halloween costume, maybe a stage name, TBD.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../ci-img/2026-04-07-klaus-gnomi.jpeg&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;wmed relative|center&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Also, since I’m here and it’s 2026, here’s an AI slop rendering of Klaus Gnomi:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;../../ci-img/2026-04-07-klaus-gnomi-ai-gen.jpeg&quot; width=&quot;auto&quot; height=&quot;auto&quot; alt=&quot;wmed relative|center&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;(Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid0iTngNJe599PDE7JL67xNg4bRK36px7R8XtFhHK63ShPJ7Kf3hWCAqnpmPubEsg4Cl&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;FB&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This war is opaque to a degree I would never have thought possible. I’m amazed by that, but even more amazed by how few people seem to recognize that fact as such. The clearest expression of it I’ve seen is scattered people half-joking that Iran has become a more reliable source that the US government.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This opacity is understandable in Iran, a country run by religious fanatics. It’s harder to understand in Israel, which — even under attack — seems like it should be a more open society. It both is and isn’t surprising that various Gulf States have gone so quiet. But it’s much harder to understand in the US, because Trump &amp;#x26; Co have only had a &lt;em&gt;year&lt;/em&gt; to bludgeon our institutions into submission.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Basically, we have no idea what’s going on. Even very basic info about events, casualties, the scale and location of accumulating destruction, the conditions of our own and others’ armed forces, and the war’s impact on cities and peoples have all been relegated to the rumor mills of social media.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It’s as if “the media” we took for granted for most of the last century has ceased to exist. And if the nearly universal failure to note that disappearance is any indication, it &lt;em&gt;has&lt;/em&gt; ceased to exist where it matters most — in the public’s mind.&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;[Orig to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facebook.com/tbyfield/posts/pfbid0YCAweyqk5gCgADs4FdkhHLKBZiVng3Td67mhmS5BDpeL2XbKvvzGdnJrj4scTLvQl&quot; class=&quot;external&quot;&gt;FB&lt;svg aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot; class=&quot;external-icon&quot; style=&quot;max-width:0.8em;max-height:0.8em&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 512 512&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M320 0H288V64h32 82.7L201.4 265.4 178.7 288 224 333.3l22.6-22.6L448 109.3V192v32h64V192 32 0H480 320zM32 32H0V64 480v32H32 456h32V480 352 320H424v32 96H64V96h96 32V32H160 32z&quot;&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The “Alt National Park Service” account is asking people to contact “companies [that] built their entire business on these lands” to “say out loud.” A few thoughts:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;First, the traditional assumption is that Congress is supposed to stop this kind of overreach. They haven’t been doing that, and indeed the Dems show &lt;em&gt;no sign whatsoever&lt;/em&gt; that they intend to address the rampant destruction Trump &amp;#x26; Co are causing — to halt it by hook or by crook, to counter-attack those responsible, to develop any semblance of a real-time strategy to hold these crimes to account &lt;em&gt;now&lt;/em&gt;, to effectively punish the culprits, or to build and fund new, more durable agencies. None. The only thing we hear is mummery about “investigations,” which is barely a step above “future historians.” When this “Alt National Park Service” account encourages you to contact companies and trade associations, they’re tacitly endorsing the idea that actual representative democracy does not and cannot work. That doesn’t seem like a very well-thought-out stance for civil servants to take.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Second, the list of “companies” is such a random-ass grab bag that I think it tells us quite a bit about this “Alt National Park Service” account.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;REI has morphed into a tightly controlled, aggressively anti-labor outfit&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;North Face is owned by VF Corporation, which controls about half of the US backpack-oriented business (incl. JanSport and Timberland)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Columbia and Vail are publicly traded&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Alterra and Vail are chain of ski resorts, one publicly traded one private equity–owned&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Orvis is family-owned, and Sitka is owned by the private company that brought you Gore-Tex&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;the National Ski Areas Association&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So, let’s see, we’re supposed to contact &lt;em&gt;two corporate ski resort chains&lt;/em&gt; and a &lt;em&gt;ski resort trade organization&lt;/em&gt; to pressure Trump not to…open up wilderness for development? Cool story, bro — pass the bong.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Third, their first advice is to cut-and-paste your objections into AI customer chats because “a handful of emails does nothing.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These “Alt” and “Rogue” federal agency accounts are fine, but I wouldn’t set much store in them. Have you ever heard a word about who runs them, how they’re run, how research is conducted and decisions are made? I’m looking for signs of movements, not of influencers. And, no, don’t try to say they can’t reveal any of that because they’re so frightened of the regime — that’s just romanticized nonsense.&lt;/p&gt;

